# 4. Director scope

For AI-6D, the Director edits **Storyboard shots**, not the screenplay.

Do not let this phase modify:

* project story text
* Scene story text
* Scene cast definition
* project characters
* character appearance
* project style

Those belong to other product stages and future specialist Agents.

The first Director Proposal should operate only within one Scene.

Support two scopes:

```text
shot
scene
```

### Shot scope

The Director receives:

* selected shot
* its Scene
* nearby shots for continuity/context

It may redesign the selected shot and, when creatively necessary, propose an adjacent shot insertion or replacement.

It must not silently redesign unrelated Scenes.

### Scene scope

The Director receives the current Scene and its complete Shot sequence.

It may propose:

* update an existing Shot
* insert a Shot
* delete a Shot
* reorder Shots

Only inside that Scene.

No cross-Scene operation in AI-6D.

---

# 5. Give the Director useful context, not the entire project dump

Build a dedicated Director storyboard context from the real project.

Include only information useful for directing the current Scene:

* project style / aspect ratio where relevant
* Scene story text
* Scene cast
* current Shot order
* stable existing Shot IDs
* each Shot's current editable creative fields
* selected Shot
* neighboring Shots
* current target durations
* enough generation state to understand whether a Shot already has assets

Do not send:

* base64 images
* giant asset histories
* full video URLs unless actually necessary
* internal revision signatures
* Workspace implementation details
* unrelated Scenes

The Director should understand the movie context without receiving an enormous project serialization.

---

# 6. Introduce a small semantic proposal schema

Create a small internal semantic layer between the LLM and ChangeOperation.

This is **not another project mutation model**.

It is only the Director's structured creative intention.

Conceptually something like:

```python
DirectorProposalIntent
DirectorProposalItem
```

A proposal item can represent:

```text
update_shot
insert_shot
delete_shot
reorder_shot
```

A semantic item may contain information such as:

```text
target existing shot
creative reason
new description
new duration
characters used
desired insertion position
desired move position
```

The exact dataclass/Pydantic organization should follow current project conventions.

Important:

A proposal item should have a server-generated stable `item_id`.

Do not ask the LLM to invent proposal item IDs.

This becomes useful for selective apply.

---

# 7. Keep semantic Proposal Items separate from compiled ChangeOperations

One visible Director proposal item does not necessarily equal one ChangeOperation.

For example:

```text
Director intention:
"Insert a reaction shot after Shot 02"
```

may require the deterministic compiler to produce:

```text
INSERT_SHOT
+
REORDER_SHOTS
```

depending on the actual locked AI-6A model.

The user should still see **one INSERT proposal**, not two unrelated technical operations.

Therefore:

```text
DirectorProposalItem
        ↓
compile
        ↓
one or more existing ChangeOperation
```

Do not expose revision implementation details to the user.

Reuse the current ChangeOperation model exactly.

Do not widen or redesign AI-6A ChangeOperation just to make Director output easier.

---

# 8. Restrict Director output to what AI-6A can actually apply

Inspect the real revision operation models.

If the current `UpdateShot` only supports a limited field set such as:

```text
description
characters
target_duration_s
```

then AI-6D Director proposals must stay within that supported set.

Do not silently expand AI-6A to camera, lighting, motion, prompt, generated assets, etc. as part of this phase.

If some UI quick action such as “composition” cannot yet map to a safe ChangeOperation, let the Director express it through the currently supported creative description rather than broadening the revision system.

Document the limitation.

---

# 9. Validate LLM output aggressively but simply

The Director output is untrusted model output.

Before compiling it:

* target Scene must exist
* target Shot must exist
* target Shot must belong to the allowed Scene
* Scene-scope proposal cannot touch another Scene
* characters must come from the Scene's existing cast
* inserted Shot must not invent a permanent Shot ID
* delete/update/reorder targets must be valid
* contradictory operations should be rejected
* empty or nonsensical proposals should fail cleanly

Do not let invalid model output reach ChangeSet Preview.

If the current Agnes client already exposes a clean structured/tool-call path, it is reasonable to use it.

If it does not, do not redesign the whole Agnes client for this phase.

A strict JSON schema + parse/validate + one repair attempt is acceptable.

The important property is:

```text
model output
≠
trusted mutation
```

---

# 10. Automatically create a Director Creative Workspace

A valid Director Proposal must never preview against canonical data directly.

After Agnes returns a valid semantic proposal:

```text
canonical project
        ↓
create Creative Workspace
        ↓
compile intentions against workspace working project
        ↓
preview ChangeSet
```

Use the existing AI-6A Workspace creation mechanism.

Suggested human-readable name:

```text
Director · Story 02 · Pacing
```

or another short title derived safely from the request.

Do not make the LLM responsible for Workspace IDs.

A Proposal should remember:

* `proposal_id`
* project
* Director
* scope type
* scope ID
* user instruction
* proposal title / summary
* semantic proposal items
* workspace ID
* base workspace revision
* full-preview ChangeSet ID
* creation time
* status

Use the project's existing atomic persistence conventions.

A natural location is under the existing Agent project data area if that matches the current implementation.

Do not introduce a database.

---

# 11. Proposal generation should not create garbage Workspaces

Do not create the Workspace before the model response is successfully parsed and validated.

Preferred order:

```text
read canonical context
↓
call Director
↓
parse + validate semantic proposal
↓
if valid:
    create Director Workspace
↓
compile
↓
preview ChangeSet
```

If Agnes fails or returns invalid content after the allowed repair attempt:

* return a clear proposal-generation failure
* do not modify canonical
* do not leave a meaningless Workspace behind

---

# 12. Reuse AI-6A Preview exactly

The full Proposal should be previewed through the existing ChangeSet engine.

Do not reproduce validation, diff, or impact logic inside Agent code.

The Proposal should receive the real:

* validation result
* structural diff
* impact report
* warnings
* preview ChangeSet ID

from AI-6A.

The Agent layer proposes creative intent.

The Revision layer decides whether that intent is structurally valid and what it affects.

---

# 13. Selective Apply must be real

This is one of the most important parts of AI-6D.

Suppose the Director proposes:

```text
A
B
C
D
```

and the initial full preview creates:

```text
ChangeSet #1 = A+B+C+D
```

If the user selects only:

```text
A + C
```

DO NOT apply ChangeSet #1.

Instead:

```text
selected Proposal Items A+C
↓
compile selected subset again
↓
preview again
↓
create ChangeSet #2
↓
recompute validation/diff/impact/warnings
↓
user confirms
↓
apply ChangeSet #2
```

The selected preview must be a real new AI-6A preview.

Do not fake selective apply in frontend state.

Do not remove unselected operations from a previously persisted ChangeSet in memory.

Do not send arbitrary after-values directly to the Apply endpoint.

Apply must continue to use the existing AI-6A rule:

```text
apply by changeset_id
```

---

# 14. Select by semantic proposal item

Selective review should preferably operate on:

```text
proposal item_id
```

rather than blindly selecting raw ChangeOperation indexes.

Reason:

one semantic Director change may compile to multiple deterministic ChangeOperations.

The backend should know which compiled operations belong to each proposal item.

This allows the UI to present:

```text
INSERT reaction shot
UPDATE Shot 02
DELETE Shot 04
```

instead of implementation details such as:

```text
INSERT_SHOT
REORDER_SHOTS
```

as two separate creative decisions.

Adapt the current `DirectorProposalOverlay` with the smallest change needed to support this.

---

# 15. KEEP is derived UI state

Do not create a new `KEEP_SHOT` ChangeOperation.

A Shot is visually `KEEP` when no selected Proposal Item affects it.

KEEP is a review presentation concept, not a persisted revision operation.

---

# 16. Proposal persistence and refresh

A real Proposal should survive a frontend refresh.

Persist enough information to retrieve the active proposal again.

Provide the smallest API surface needed to:

* create Proposal
* fetch Proposal
* optionally list recent Director proposals for a scope
* preview a selected subset
* mark dismissed/applied if appropriate

Follow current router/API conventions.

Do not build a full “Proposal management center”.

That is outside AI-6D.

---

# 17. Concurrency and stale Proposal protection

A Proposal is created against a specific Workspace revision.

Store that revision.

If the same Workspace changes before the Proposal is applied, do not silently reapply old assumptions.

Return a real conflict/stale state and ask the user to regenerate or review again.

Continue relying on AI-6A revision checks.

Do not weaken:

* before-value checks
* workspace revision checks
* signatures
* atomic apply

Similarly, if canonical has moved since the Proposal's Director Workspace was created, do not pretend the Proposal represents the latest canonical storyboard.

Use existing Workspace stale/base-signature information if available and expose a warning.

The Workspace remains a valid historical creative branch; the UI just needs to be honest about its base.

